Overview
You will write four Java programs (Program1, Program2, Program3, and Program4) to explore different approaches to handling arrays of various types.

Instructions

Program1: Using an Object Array
Write a Java program named Program1.
Implement a printArray() method that takes an array of Object as a parameter and prints its elements.
In the main method, initialize and test the method with the following arrays:
Integer[] intArry = {1, 2, 3, 4, 5};
Double[] doubArry = {1.1, 2.2, 3.3, 4.4};
Character[] charArry = {'H', 'E', 'L', 'L', 'O'};
String[] strArry = {"once", "upon", "a", "time"};
printArray(intArry);
printArray(doubArry);
printArray(charArry);
printArray(strArry);
Compile and run your program.


Program2: Overloaded Methods
Write a Java program named Program2.
Implement four versions of printArray(), one for each data type: Integer[], Double[], Character[], and String[].
Ensure that each method correctly prints the elements of its respective array.
Compile and run your program.

Program3: Generic Method
Write a Java program named Program3.
Implement a single generic version of printArray() that supports different data types while ensuring type safety.
Use Java generics to write a method that prints any type of array.
Compile and run your program.

Program4: Finding the Maximum Value
Write a Java program named Program4.
Implement a method getMax() that takes an array of Comparable elements and returns the maximum element.
public static Comparable getMax(Comparable[] anArray)
In the main method, test getMax() using the provided arrays:
System.out.println("Max Integer is: " + getMax(intArry));
System.out.println("Max Double is: " + getMax(doubArry));
System.out.println("Max Character is: " + getMax(charArry));
System.out.println("Max String is: " + getMax(strArry));
Compile and run your program.

